1
00:00:00,295 --> 00:00:05,035
Three decisions shape every tokenizer. First, the method: the

2
00:00:05,035 --> 00:00:08,755
algorithm that learns where to cut. Byte pair encoding

3
00:00:08,755 --> 00:00:12,685
for the GPT family. WordPiece for BERT. They aim

4
00:00:12,685 --> 00:00:14,835
at the same goal and reach it in different

5
00:00:14,835 --> 00:00:15,571
ways.

6
00:00:17,127 --> 00:00:20,107
Second, the parameters, and the most visible one is

7
00:00:20,107 --> 00:00:23,197
vocabulary size. These are the real numbers from the

8
00:00:23,197 --> 00:00:27,187
book. BERT uncased holds thirty thousand five hundred and

9
00:00:27,187 --> 00:00:31,657
twenty two entries. GPT-2 holds fifty thousand two hundred

10
00:00:31,657 --> 00:00:35,327
and fifty seven. GPT-4 holds a little over one

11
00:00:35,327 --> 00:00:36,438
hundred thousand.

12
00:00:36,710 --> 00:00:40,000
Third, the data the tokenizer is trained on. Same

13
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,160
method, same size, but trained on code instead of

14
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:46,160
prose, and you get a different vocabulary. So a

15
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:48,560
method name alone can never tell you how a

16
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:49,884
word will be split.

17
00:00:49,982 --> 00:00:55,842
Every tokenizer also reserves special tokens. Unknown, padding, beginning

18
00:00:55,842 --> 00:00:59,912
and end of text, separator, mask. These are not

19
00:00:59,912 --> 00:01:02,678
words. They are instructions to the model.

20
00:01:03,281 --> 00:01:06,061
Now the real thing. The book runs this next

21
00:01:06,061 --> 00:01:09,601
example on the Phi-3 tokenizer, which reuses the Llama

22
00:01:09,601 --> 00:01:12,861
2 vocabulary and adds chat tokens. Here is our

23
00:01:12,861 --> 00:01:14,578
word: apologizing.

24
00:01:14,841 --> 00:01:17,601
The tokenizer does not keep it whole, and it

25
00:01:17,601 --> 00:01:20,391
does not fall back to letters. It returns two

26
00:01:20,391 --> 00:01:24,901
pieces: apolog, and izing. Neither piece is a word

27
00:01:24,901 --> 00:01:25,978
on its own.

28
00:01:26,721 --> 00:01:29,731
Each piece is an entry in the vocabulary, and

29
00:01:29,731 --> 00:01:33,491
each entry has an integer. Apolog is twenty seven

30
00:01:33,491 --> 00:01:37,171
thousand seven hundred and forty six. Izing is five

31
00:01:37,171 --> 00:01:40,031
thousand two hundred and eighty one. Those are the

32
00:01:40,031 --> 00:01:41,838
numbers the model receives.

33
00:01:42,594 --> 00:01:45,004
And there is a detail you cannot see in

34
00:01:45,004 --> 00:01:48,814
ordinary text. Izing carries a hidden character at its

35
00:01:48,814 --> 00:01:52,528
start, meaning: I am joined to what came before.

36
00:01:52,528 --> 00:01:54,894
A token without that marker is read as starting

37
00:01:54,894 --> 00:01:58,390
a new word. Whitespace is part of the encoding,

38
00:01:58,390 --> 00:01:59,904
not part of the string.

39
00:02:00,628 --> 00:02:04,498
The same machinery runs in reverse. The model's output

40
00:02:04,498 --> 00:02:07,531
is not text either. It is more integers.

41
00:02:07,751 --> 00:02:11,341
Three thousand three hundred and twenty three. Six hundred

42
00:02:11,341 --> 00:02:13,903
and twenty two. Look them up and you get

43
00:02:13,903 --> 00:02:18,431
Sub, and ject. Decoded together, they read Subject.

44
00:02:18,660 --> 00:02:21,290
So the tokenizer stands on both sides of the

45
00:02:21,290 --> 00:02:25,090
model. Text to IDs on the way in. IDs

46
00:02:25,090 --> 00:02:27,690
back to text on the way out. That is

47
00:02:27,690 --> 00:02:30,971
why the same component appears twice in the pipeline.
